      "content": "# Coding Standards\n\nRead this document before generating any Python code for a Universal Extension. These rules apply to all generated code \u2014 actions, utilities, helpers, and formatters.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OOP Pattern \u2014 Wrap Related Functions in Classes\n\nAll generated code follows an OOP pattern. Related functions are grouped into classes, state is held as instance attributes, and private helpers use `self` instead of receiving state as explicit arguments.\n\n```python\n# Good \u2014 class groups state and behaviour together\nclass RunPipeline:\n    def __init__(self, input_data: InputFields) -> None:\n        self._input = input_data\n        self._client = PipelineClient(input_data)\n        self._output_fields = OutputFields()\n\n    def execute(self) -> ActionOutput:\n        result = self._submit()\n        self._output_fields.update(pipeline_state=result.state.name)\n        return ActionOutput(pipeline_run_id=result.name)\n\n    def _submit(self) -> PipelineResult:\n        return self._client.submit(\n            display_name=self._input.display_name,\n            template_path=self._input.template_path,\n        )\n\n# Bad \u2014 module-level function threading state through arguments\ndef run_pipeline(input_data: InputFields) -> ActionOutput:\n    credentials = _build_credentials(input_data)\n    client = _build_client(credentials, input_data.project_id, input_data.region)\n    result = _submit(client, input_data.display_name, input_data.template_path)\n    ...\n```\n\nSee `actions.md` for the full action class pattern and `credentials.md` for the credentials handler pattern.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Constants as Class Attributes\n\nConstants belong to the class that uses them. Module-level constants are a last resort reserved for values that are genuinely shared across multiple unrelated classes.\n\n```python\n# Good \u2014 scoped to the class that uses them\nclass PipelineFormatter:\n    _TABLE_FMT = \"rounded_outline\"\n    _SUMMARY_HEADERS = [\"Field\", \"Value\"]\n\n# Bad \u2014 floating at module level\n_TABLE_FMT = \"rounded_outline\"\n_SUMMARY_HEADERS = [\"Field\", \"Value\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Duplicate String Literals \u2192 StrEnum\n\nAny string value repeated 3 or more times becomes a `StrEnum` member. This prevents SonarQube S1192 and makes renaming a single-line change. `StrEnum` values behave as plain strings in all comparison and serialization contexts.\n\n```python\nfrom enum import StrEnum\n\nclass PipelineState(StrEnum):\n    RUNNING = \"PIPELINE_STATE_RUNNING\"\n    SUCCEEDED = \"PIPELINE_STATE_SUCCEEDED\"\n    FAILED = \"PIPELINE_STATE_FAILED\"\n    CANCELLED = \"PIPELINE_STATE_CANCELLED\"\n\nclass RunPipeline:\n    def _is_terminal(self, state: str) -> bool:\n        return state in (PipelineState.SUCCEEDED, PipelineState.FAILED, PipelineState.CANCELLED)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. State Values from the API, Not Hardcoded\n\nWhen the target API returns a state, status, or identifier in its response, use that value directly. Do not hardcode strings the API will return.\n\n```python\n# Good \u2014 use what the SDK returned\nself._output_fields.update(pipeline_state=result.state.name)\n\n# Bad \u2014 hardcoded, and often wrong (submit returns PENDING, not RUNNING)\nself._output_fields.update(pipeline_state=\"PIPELINE_STATE_RUNNING\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Redundant Exception Subclasses in except Tuples\n\nNever list a subclass alongside its parent in an `except` tuple \u2014 the parent already catches it. This eliminates SonarQube S1181.\n\n```python\n# Good \u2014 minimal, non-redundant set\nexcept (GoogleAuthError, OSError, GoogleAPICallError) as exc:\n\n# Bad \u2014 Unauthenticated, Forbidden, NotFound, DeadlineExceeded are GoogleAPICallError subclasses;\n#       ConnectionError is an OSError subclass \u2014 all redundant\nexcept (GoogleAuthError, Unauthenticated, Forbidden, NotFound,\n        DeadlineExceeded, ConnectionError, OSError, GoogleAPICallError) as exc:\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Logging Uses %s Interpolation \u2014 All Other Strings Use f-strings\n\nThe `logging` module evaluates format strings lazily \u2014 the string is only constructed if the log level is active. f-strings evaluate at the call site regardless of log level. Use `%s` (and `%d`, `%r`, etc.) inside `logging` calls; use f-strings everywhere else.\n\n```python\n# Good \u2014 lazy evaluation; string is built only if INFO is enabled\nlogger.info(\"Submitting pipeline job: display_name=%s, region=%s\", display_name, region)\nlogger.error(\"Job failed after %d retries: %s\", retries, error_message)\n\n# Bad \u2014 forces string construction even when INFO is disabled\nlogger.info(f\"Submitting pipeline job: display_name={display_name}, region={region}\")\n\n# Good \u2014 f-string is correct outside logging\nraise ServiceConnectionError(f\"Failed to connect to {endpoint} after {retries} retries\")\nerror_detail = f\"Job {job_id} entered state {state} after {elapsed:.1f}s\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. No Path Guards on UAC-Resolved Fields\n\nUAC resolves and validates required template fields (file paths, scripts) before the extension runs. Defensive `Path(x).exists()` guards on those fields are unnecessary noise.\n\n```python\n# Good \u2014 pass directly; UAC has already resolved the path\nwith open(input_data.script_path) as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Bad \u2014 UAC guarantees this file exists for required fields\nif not Path(input_data.script_path).exists():\n    raise DataValidationError(f\"Script not found: {input_data.script_path}\")\nwith open(input_data.script_path) as f:\n    content = f.read()\n```\n\nApply this rule only to fields that are **required** in the template. Optional fields (where the user may leave them blank) can still be guarded.\n",
      "numLines": 145,
      "startLine": 1,
      "totalLines": 145
    }
  },
